The requested output assert code is actually embedded within the existing code. Here is the complete method:

```cs
using System.IO;
using System.Diagnostics;

void LoadTemplates(string fileName)
{
	// @@@ the template file exists
    Debug.Assert(File.Exists(fileName),"The Template file does not exist");

    // Code to load templates continues here
}
```

Note: The Debug.Assert checks a Boolean condition. In this context, it means it ensures that the file exists. If the file does not exist, it throws an exception, and you can configure the output of the exception or event.--------------------Here is a C# solution program that extracts and prints all Debug.Assert statements from the given code:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        string code = File.ReadAllText("source.cs"); //replace with your source file
        Regex regex = new Regex(@"// @@@ (.*)");
        var matches = regex.Matches(code);

        foreach (Match match in matches)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Debug.Assert(true, \"{match.Groups[1].Value}\");");
        }
    }
}
```

Replace `"source.cs"` with your file's name. It will output all Debug.Assert statements found in source code.

Please, keep in mind that you'll need to call `Debug.Assert` yourself in your app, e.g. `Debug.Assert(templatesFileExist, "the template file exists");` and enable `TRACE` and `DEBUG` levels in your project's configuration to see the `Debug.Assert` messages.

Don't forget to include `using System.Diagnostics;` at the top of the file.

This program reads the code from a source file, and searches for all lines starting with // @@@ and everything after that. It then prints out the `Debug.Assert` statement with the text of the comment as the assertion message. Of course, you'd still be responsible for writing and executing code like `Debug.Assert(templatesFileExist, "the template file exists")` in your application.--------------------This problem involves parsing the given C# code in search of comments and printing the part which matches the regular expression "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);". Here is a C# program which will output the lines containing assert statements:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;
using System.IO;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string text = File.ReadAllText("yourFile.cs");

        foreach (Match match in Regex.Matches(text, @"//(?:@@@\s?)(.*)|\r\n(?:@@@\s?)(.*)\r\n", RegexOptions.Singleline))
        {
            Console.WriteLine(match.Value);
        }
    }
}
```

In this program, the regular expression "(?:@@@\s?)(.*)|\r\n(?:@@@\s?)(.*)\r\n" is used to search string for the content between "@@@" and line breaks. The string is read from a file - "yourFile.cs". 

Make sure to replace "yourFile.cs" with the actual name of your C# file.

This program will print the lines containing "Debug.Assert(...);".

Note: This code will only work if the file you are searching is UTF-8 encoded. 

Please replace the name of your desired csharp file with "yourFile.cs", or you can provide the actual path of the filename which needs to be read.--------------------Here is the appropriate csharp code:

```cs
using System.IO;
using System.Diagnostics;

void LoadTemplates(string fileName)
{
	bool templatesFileExist = File.Exists(fileName);
	Debug.Assert(templatesFileExist, "The template file does not exist."); 
}
```

Debug.Assert(templatesFileExist, "The template file does not exist."); is the assertion statement that makes sure the template file exists. If the file does not exist, it will result in a break in the execution of the application with the message "The template file does not exist.".--------------------The output for your desired assertion for the code block would be:

```cs
System.Diagnostics.Debug.Assert(templatesFileExist);
```

This assertion is checking if the boolean `templatesFileExist` is `true`, indicating that the templates file exists. If the file does not exist, this is likely a condition that could cause the program to fail or behave unexpectedly. 

So, by using this assertion, we can ensure that the file exists at any time the method or block is called. If `fileName` was invalid or the file was not accessible for some reason, the assertion will catch it and alert the developer or tester.--------------------Here's how the above csharp code could be extended with output assert statements:

```cs
using System.IO;
using System.Diagnostics;

void LoadTemplates(string fileName)
{
	bool templatesFileExist = File.Exists(fileName);
	Debug.Assert(templatesFileExist, "The template file does not exist");
	// The template file exists
}

```--------------------In the given code, a Debug.Assert statement has been added at line 3, which checks if a template file exists based on user input.

Here is the corresponding Assert statement for the comment starting with "@@@":

```cs
Debug.Assert(templatesFileExist);
```

This code will fail if templatesFileExist is false (i.e., if the template file does not exist). If it is true (i.e., the template file exists), the Assert statement is a no-op, meaning it does nothing. This effectively serves as a way to verify that the template file exists before trying to load it.

Thus, debugging for this case can be achieved by adding corresponding Assert statements before the function that might fail due to the situation in question. For example, suppose that LoadTemplates function is used in a method:

```cs
void RunBusinessLogic()
{
    // some code here

    Debug.Assert(File.Exists("templateFile.txt"));  // here also could be a user provided file
    LoadTemplates("templateFile.txt");

    // rest of the business logic
}
```
In this case, if there exists no templateFile.txt file in the run directory, the assertion will cause the application to stop. This will help to debug why no such file is being created in a given part of the code.--------------------The provided code checks whether a file exists or not so it should print "Debug.Assert(templatesFileExist);"

Here's how this would look in your C# code:

```cs
using System.Diagnostics;
using System.IO;

void LoadTemplates(string fileName)
{
	bool templatesFileExist = File.Exists(fileName);
	// @@@ the template file exists
	Debug.Assert(templatesFileExist); // will fail if templatesFileExist is false
}

```

You can change the assertion to not fail if the templates file does not exist by replacing `Debug.Assert(templatesFileExist);` with `Debug.Assert(templatesFileExist, "The template file does not exist.");` to provide a more meaningful message in the debugger.--------------------```csharp
Debug.Assert(templatesFileExist);

```--------------------Here is a C# code snippet that will output the required debug assert statements as per the comments:

```cs
using System.IO;
using System.Diagnostics;

void LoadTemplates(string fileName)
{
	bool templatesFileExist = File.Exists(fileName);
	// @@@ the template file exists
	Debug.Assert(templatesFileExist, "The template file does not exist.");
}
```
In the given code, we are using the `System.Diagnostics.Debug.Assert` method to check if a file exists or not, and creating an error message "The template file does not exist.", if the file does not exist. Our assertion will be active if the condition "templatesFileExist" returns false, indicating that the file does not exist. Therefore, we are checking the "templatesFileExist" variable as the condition to the `Assert` method.

This part of the code is typically useful for debugging purposes. It ensures we are working with a valid file and provides useful feedback in the event that the file no longer exists. Without this check, our program would have thrown an "FileNotFoundException" if the file does not exist. So, an assertion here helps us ensure that an error is thrown only when we need to know about it.--------------------